 ## Overview
-Visibility culling is a family of acceleration techniques in volume rendering that identifies and skips the processing of voxels, primitives, or data structures that do not contribute to the final pixel color, thereby reducing computational load [S2][S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by focusing on the *visibility* of data elements relative to the camera or screen space, rather than just skipping empty space (as in [Space Skipping and Empty Volume Culling](10063-space-skipping-and-empty-volume-culling.md)) or terminating rays based on opacity (as in [Early Termination and Probabilistic Path Control](1791-early-termination-and-probabilistic-path-control.md)). While [Parallel Ray Casting with Segment Lists](2565-parallel-ray-casting-with-segment-lists.md) and [SparseLeap Ray-Casting with Occupancy Misses](9818-sparseleap-ray-casting-with-occupancy-misses.md) optimize the traversal of non-empty regions, visibility culling often operates at a coarser level (e.g., frustum, tile, or sub-volume) to avoid even initiating the traversal for occluded or out-of-view data.
+Visibility culling encompasses techniques that determine which parts of a volume dataset contribute to the final image and bypass the computation for those that do not, thereby reducing the number of samples or primitives processed per frame [G]. Unlike space skipping, which bypasses empty regions along a ray, visibility culling typically addresses occlusion by other geometry or data, or uses geometric bounds to exclude entire datasets from the view [G]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by focusing on the *visibility* of data blocks or primitives relative to the camera and other visible surfaces, rather than just the emptiness of the space between the camera and the data.
 
